% Dimensional construction of the S-state regularizer and its cadence normalization.
% Historical interpretation of the former cubic schedule is deferred until after
% the reduced physical-MSE derivation.

\subsection{Dimensionally consistent base and cadence normalization}
\label{sec:adaptation-cadence-interpretation}

Before optimizing physical displacement MSE, it is useful to establish the
natural dimensions of the integral-state pseudo-measurement and the effect of
changing its cadence.  Let $a_R$ denote an acceleration scale chosen for the
regularizer.  Because $S$ is the third time integral of acceleration, its
natural dimensional scale is
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,\mathrm{base}}
 = C_R a_R\tau^3.
\label{eq:adapt-cadence-cubic-base}
\end{equation}
Here $C_R$ is dimensionless.  This equation is a dimensional construction only:
it does not yet decide which acceleration scale $a_R$ is physically appropriate,
nor does it claim MSE optimality.

Suppose Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-cadence-cubic-base} defines the desired
pseudo-measurement standard deviation at a reference update period $T_{S,0}$.
Because one $S=0$ update with variance $R_S=r_S^2$ is applied every $T_S$
seconds, its continuous-equivalent information rate is proportional to
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal I_S \propto \frac{1}{r_S^2T_S}.
\label{eq:adapt-cadence-information-rate}
\end{equation}
If $T_S$ increases while $r_S$ is left at the same base value, the regularizer
is applied less frequently and its information rate falls.  Preserving the
information rate represented by the base value therefore requires
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}^2 T_S
 = r_{S,\mathrm{base}}^2 T_{S,0},
\label{eq:adapt-cadence-rate-match}
\end{equation}
which gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
 = r_{S,\mathrm{base}}
 \sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}}.}
\label{eq:adapt-cadence-rate-normalization}
\end{equation}
Thus, when pseudo-measurements are less frequent, each individual update must be
stronger than the unnormalized base value by the square-root cadence factor.
This does not make absolute regularization stronger as the sea period grows;
$r_S$ can still increase strongly with $\tau$.  The square root only preserves
information rate while the update interval changes.

With the sea-scaled cadence $T_S=c_T\tau$ away from clamps,
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:adapt-cadence-cubic-base} and
\eqref{eq:adapt-cadence-rate-normalization} give
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
 = C_R a_R
   \sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{c_T}}\,\tau^{5/2}.}
\label{eq:adapt-cadence-five-halves}
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1}{r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}^2T_S}
 \propto \frac{1}{a_R^2\tau^6}.
\label{eq:adapt-cadence-tau-minus-six}
\end{equation}
The $5/2$ exponent is therefore the per-update consequence of combining a
third-integral dimensional scale with a cadence proportional to $\tau$.  This
construction fixes neither the correct amplitude dependence nor the physical
bias--distortion optimum.  Those are supplied by the reduced-MSE analysis in
the following sections; only afterward is the historical
$a_R=\sigma_{aw}$ implementation interpreted as the legacy cubic schedule.